MLOps: Segment by Segment

Segment 1: What MLOps Is

MLOps (Machine Learning Operations) is the practice of applying
DevOps-style discipline to machine learning systems —
the processes and tooling that let teams
build,
deploy,
monitor, and
maintain ML models reliably in production,
instead of leaving them as one-off notebooks only one person understands.

Segment 2: Why It's Needed

Regular software has one moving part that changes:
code. ML systems have three: code, data, and the trained model itself —
and each can change independently and silently break things.
Data drifts over time,
the real world shifts away from what the model was trained on,
and a model that looked great in testing can quietly degrade in
production with no one noticing until something goes wrong downstream.

Segment 3: The Core Components
Versioning — tracking not just code, but also datasets and trained models,
 so you can always trace which data + code produced which model.
Pipelines —
automated workflows for data validation,
training,
evaluation,
and deployment, so shipping a new model doesn't require manual, error-prone steps each time.
Note: Jenkins, Concourse, GITHUB Actions
Monitoring — watching production models for drift,
data changes,
and accuracy decay,
not just server uptime and latency.
Governance — reproducibility and rollback, so a bad model version can be reverted quickly.

Segment 4: Common Tools by Job
Experiment tracking: MLflow, Weights & Biases
Feature store: Feast, Tecton
Model registry: MLflow Registry, SageMaker Model Registry
Pipeline orchestration: Kubeflow, Airflow, SageMaker Pipelines, Vertex AI Pipelines
Model serving: BentoML, Seldon, or built-in cloud serving
All-in-one platforms: AWS SageMaker, Google Vertex AI, Azure ML

Segment 5: A Typical Workflow
Data is validated and versioned.
A training pipeline runs (scheduled or triggered by new data).
The new model is evaluated against a held-out set and compared to the current production model.
If it passes, it's promoted through staging into production.
Production traffic is monitored for drift or degraded performance,
with alerts or automated rollback if things go wrong.